  "receipts": [
    {
      "receiptId": "steak-pudding.html-1",
      "receiptSource": "Original Receipt in \u0027\u003Ci\u003EThe Art of Cookery, Made Plain and Easy\u003C/i\u003E\u0027 by Hannah Glasse, 1747 (\u003Ca href=\u0022references.html#glasse-1747\u0022\u003E\u003Cb\u003EGlasse 1747\u003C/b\u003E\u003C/a\u003E);",
      "receiptText": "\u003Cb\u003EA steak pudding.\u003C/b\u003E\u003Cbr\u003E\nMAKE a good crust, with suet shred fine with flour, and mix it up with cold water. Season it with a little salt, and make a pretty stiff crust, about two pounds of suet to a quarter of a peck of flour. Let your steaks be either beef or mutton, well seasoned with pepper and salt, make it up as you do an apple-pudding, tie it in a cloth, and put it into the water boiling. If it be a large pudding, it will take five hours; a small one, three hours. This is the best crust for an apple-pudding. Pigeons eat well this way.\u003Cbr\u003E"
    }
  ],
